{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner planning correction of R1 revision 3, exact head 4b7f77eb3955a9f81a1a502b561f20c86eb7dbc5. I read the complete two-file revision against the fully read revision 2, including all 395 changed main-note lines and the full correction matrix. The main note is 1407 lines,81298 bytes,SHA-256808aafce1b7fc0c8a1d2935acbd25008b036236593b695a3731f238145514c3a; the matrix is43 lines,9982 bytes,SHA-256711e4bd8f15a7699f7131d30d536aeefa196913bda139c17111e322b50b41b94. The unchanged admission inventory was already read in full. The revision diff passes whitespace checking. This is planning correction, not an independent verdict or source authorization.\n\nThe one-new-item identity rule, explicit creator confirmation, terminating message classes, run-time namespace trust apart from replay, report attribution of the actual commitment, and precise opening/revision rules address the principal previous ambiguities. Four focused corrections remain before filing the revised design.\n\n1. Do not turn loss of the receiver into proof that a request never took effect. Section7.4 says wrong-incarnation/not-found means no receiver exists that could decide, and after three attempts records undeliverable and runs the terminal clause. Counterexample: the original incarnation admitted a request, its result was lost, and its store then disappeared or was replaced provisionally. The present routing refusal cannot settle that original outcome. Distinguish current delivery impossibility from original effect settlement. A recorded delivery-unavailable diagnosis may stop ordinary retransmission, but must preserve correlation, unknown-effect duties, evidence, pins and fences until reconciliation; no terminal clause may release a publication/resource reservation or initiate a substitute side effect on that evidence alone. State the actual durable proof needed for a safely never-dispatched request, and the exact recovery when a late result or restored original history appears